---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/evidence.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const c = data.counts;
const personFiles = data.files.filter(f => f.kind === 'person');
const refFiles = data.files.filter(f => f.kind === 'ref');
const linked = data.people.filter(p => p.register_id);
const ambiguous = data.people.filter(p => p.ambiguous);
---
<Base title="What has actually been read"
  subtitle={c.records + ' records, about ' + c.people + ' people, from ' + c.person_files + ' files'}
  lede="Every person below has a record behind them that somebody opened, transcribed and filed. This page exists because for three days none of that reached the site.">

  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.people}</b><span>People with a record</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.records}</b><span>Records</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.files}</b><span>Evidence files</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.linked_to_register}</b><span>Matched to the register</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The fault this page was built to close <span class="tag disp">17 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      This archive kept twenty-one evidence files in <code>data/</code> &mdash; censuses, the
      Gedenkbuch, the 1939 Register, passenger lists, court files. <strong>Not one build script read
      any of them.</strong> The register was assembled from the MyHeritage export and
      <code>corrections.json</code> and nothing else. Everything a record actually said reached this
      site only because a human retyped it into a paragraph.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is survivable while the human is paying attention and silently wrong the moment they are
      not. <strong>Nothing would have noticed</strong> if a file had been added and never surfaced, or
      if a page and a file had come to disagree. There was no gate, because there was nothing to gate.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      The clearest casualty was <strong>Samuel Israel Leibholz</strong>. The register printed his birth
      as <em>&ldquo;June 28 1877?&rdquo;</em> &mdash; with the source tree&rsquo;s question mark intact
      &mdash; while <code>data/leibholz-gedenkbuch.tsv</code> held the Bundesarchiv&rsquo;s entry
      <strong>en909753</strong>, giving 28 June 1877 at Schubin, and his death at Auschwitz on 10 April
      1943. The archive had the citation. The page showed the doubt.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What this page does not do</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>It does not rewrite the tree.</strong> The register and the charts draw the export as
      given, and that rule has not changed: the question mark on Samuel&rsquo;s birth year is still
      there, because that is what the source says. What has changed is that the record sits beside it
      instead of in a file nobody reads. Where a named source genuinely overrides the family record
      that is a <a href={base + '/method/'}>correction</a>, shown as <em>was</em> and <em>now</em>, and
      it is a different mechanism on purpose.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>People matched to a register entry</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    Name matching is exact on the name or on first-plus-last, so this number is a floor and not a
    count of everyone documented. Most of the people below are on the English side, which the
    MyHeritage export never contained at all.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>In the register as</th><th>Records read</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{linked.map(p => (
      <tr>
        <td><strong>{p.name}</strong></td>
        <td class="small">{p.register_name} <span class="muted">#{p.register_id}</span></td>
        <td class="small">{p.records.map(r => r.title).join(' · ')}</td>
      </tr>
    ))}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  {ambiguous.length > 0 && (
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Names this builder refuses to resolve <span class="tag disp">Not linked</span></h3>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>A name match is not a person match</strong>, and the first version of this builder
      proved it on its own data. The register holds a <em>Kurt Luwinski</em> who died in 1995 — a
      surname-index entry — and a <em>Kurt Israel Luwinski</em>, 1912 to 1968, who is the man this
      archive is about. Matching the court files on the bare name hung three divorce actions on the
      wrong one. The builder now pools every candidate before deciding, and <strong>where more than
      one person answers to a name it links neither and says so here.</strong>
    </p>
    {ambiguous.map(p => (
      <p class="small">
        <strong>{p.name}</strong> — documented in {[...new Set(p.records.map(r => r.file))].join(', ')} —
        could be: {p.ambiguous.map(a => (a.name + ' #' + a.id + (a.died ? ', d. ' + a.died : ', no death recorded'))).join('  |  ')}
      </p>
    ))}
    <p class="small muted">
      Resolving these is a human job and is deliberately not automated. The second pair above is
      also a <strong>duplicate in the source tree</strong> that the register's own collapse list does
      not cover — one copy carries a death date and the other does not.
    </p>
  </div>
  )}

  <h2>Every person with a record behind them</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Record</th><th>What it says</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{data.people.map(p => p.records.map((r, i) => (
      <tr>
        <td>{i === 0 ? <strong>{p.name}</strong> : <span class="muted small">&nbsp;</span>}</td>
        <td class="small">{r.title}<br /><span class="muted">{r.source}</span></td>
        <td class="small muted">{r.detail}</td>
      </tr>
    )))}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>The files themselves</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What it is</th><th>Source</th><th>Rows</th><th>People</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{data.files.map(f => (
      <tr>
        <td><code class="small">{f.file}</code></td>
        <td class="small">{f.title}</td>
        <td class="small muted">{f.source}</td>
        <td>{f.rows}</td>
        <td>{f.kind === 'person' ? f.people : <span class="muted small">by reference</span>}</td>
      </tr>
    ))}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Four files are keyed by reference and not by person</h3>
    <p class="small">
      {refFiles.map(f => f.file).join(', ')} &mdash; these record a search, a sweep or a set of
      archival references rather than a household. They are declared in the builder&rsquo;s manifest
      so that they are accounted for, and they attach to no individual on purpose.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The gate that now stands behind this page</h3>
    <p class="small">
      <code>tools/check-evidence.py</code> runs on every build. It refuses the build if a file appears
      in <code>data/</code> that the builder does not declare, and it refuses the build if a person
      named in an evidence file reaches the published site carrying neither a record nor a correction.
      <strong>The failure that made this page possible cannot happen again without the build stopping.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
